WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.379
On the left, one bore. Somebody drilled it once, and it has a

2
00:00:04.479 --> 00:00:08.492
real size that is not going to change while we watch it.

3
00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:12.707
On the right, everything we are ever going to know about it.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.990
The gauge is the thing in your hand. Close it

5
00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.916
onto the bore and it gives you a number.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.636
One number. Write it down.

7
00:00:21.733 --> 00:00:25.635
Now put it down, pick it up, and measure the same bore

8
00:00:25.735 --> 00:00:30.081
again. Nothing has been touched. The number is not the same.

9
00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:33.145
Twenty times. The same bore, the same

10
00:00:33.245 --> 00:00:36.306
gauge, the same afternoon, one person.

11
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.681
The bore never moved. The numbers did. That is

12
00:00:39.781 --> 00:00:43.578
the whole subject of this level, and everything after

13
00:00:43.678 --> 00:00:46.740
this is about the shape those numbers make.

14
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:52.477
Here is that shape, one reading at a time.

15
00:00:53.066 --> 00:00:58.222
This is one bore, machined once. It has exactly one size.

16
00:00:58.316 --> 00:01:02.056
One size, and nothing has been measured yet.

17
00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:07.601
Measure it once, and again, and two hundred times. The bore has not changed.

18
00:01:07.701 --> 00:01:12.130
The readings have a shape, a centre and a spread of their own.

19
00:01:12.216 --> 00:01:16.401
One true size. Seven microns of spread on the screen.

20
00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:20.444
That spread belongs to the gauge, not to the part.

21
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:25.393
So a reading is never just the part.

22
00:01:25.483 --> 00:01:28.016
It is the part, plus whatever the

23
00:01:28.116 --> 00:01:31.287
measurement process did on that occasion.

24
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:34.536
Watch the error, not the part. The part is

25
00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:37.866
fixed. The error is a draw from the gauge's

26
00:01:37.966 --> 00:01:42.203
own distribution, and it lands somewhere new every time.

27
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.928
That distribution is the thing this whole subject studies.

28
00:01:48.516 --> 00:01:53.433
If the reading is the part plus an error, the spread you observe must

29
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:58.304
be wider than the spread of the parts. The question is by how much.

30
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:02.092
The tempting answer is that the two spreads add.

31
00:02:02.183 --> 00:02:06.029
It is not. Independent variation adds the way

32
00:02:06.129 --> 00:02:08.923
the sides of a right triangle do.

33
00:02:09.016 --> 00:02:13.644
The variances add. The standard deviations do not.

34
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:17.900
Four point seven and one point four give four point

35
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.832
nine, not six point one. The gauge is a quarter

36
00:02:21.932 --> 00:02:25.513
of the part spread and it costs 4.3 percent.

37
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.208
Sweep the gauge from perfect to as bad as

38
00:02:30.308 --> 00:02:33.584
the parts themselves, and watch what it costs.

39
00:02:33.683 --> 00:02:37.552
A gauge at a tenth of the part spread costs half a

40
00:02:37.652 --> 00:02:42.235
percent. At a third it costs four. The curve is almost flat

41
00:02:42.335 --> 00:02:46.759
here, which is why a mediocre gauge often goes unnoticed.

42
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.842
Then it bends. A gauge as noisy as the

43
00:02:49.942 --> 00:02:53.830
parts inflates what you see by forty one percent.

44
00:02:53.916 --> 00:02:57.743
Read it the other way and it is uncomfortable. Your

45
00:02:57.843 --> 00:03:01.671
gauge may be nearly half the part spread before the

46
00:03:01.771 --> 00:03:06.368
histogram is ten percent too wide. Small effects hide easily.

47
00:03:08.166 --> 00:03:12.386
The obvious move is to measure each part several times

48
00:03:12.486 --> 00:03:15.746
and average. It works, and it has a floor.

49
00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:20.778
Averaging divides the measurement variance by the number of repeats. It

50
00:03:20.878 --> 00:03:24.900
does nothing at all to the variation between the parts, so

51
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.453
the curve falls towards the part spread and stops.

52
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:33.318
Five repeats gets you within one percent of the floor and twenty-five

53
00:03:33.418 --> 00:03:37.905
barely improves on that. So the honest question is never how good

54
00:03:38.005 --> 00:03:41.856
is my gauge. It is how big is it, compared to something.

55
00:03:41.956 --> 00:03:46.090
Level two asks whose spread we have actually been measuring.

